The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```
void cfg80211_rfkill_block_work(struct work_struct *work) {
    struct cfg80211_registered_device *rdev;
    rdev = container_of(work, struct cfg80211_registered_device, rfkill_block);
    cfg80211_shutdown_all_interfaces(rdev);
}
```
```
// In wiphy_unregister, before the fix: no cancellation of rfkill_block work
// (the line `cancel_work_sync(&rdev->rfkill_block);` is missing)
```
Reason:
The `rfkill_block` work is scheduled and can execute after `wiphy_unregister` frees the `rdev` data structures. When the work runs, it calls `cfg80211_shutdown_all_interfaces(rdev)` which accesses the already-freed memory, causing a use-after-free.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```
+	cancel_work_sync(&rdev->rfkill_block);
```
```
// Inserted in wiphy_unregister before other cancellation calls
```
Reason:
By adding `cancel_work_sync(&rdev->rfkill_block)`, the pending work is cancelled and drained before the wiphy’s resources are freed. This prevents the work from running after unregistration, eliminating the use-after-free.

